In $L C$-oscillation, maximum stored energy in inductor coil $L$ is $\frac{1}{2} L I_0^2$ and maximum stored energy in capacitor $(C)$ is $\frac{q_0^2}{2 C}$.
Since, energy is equally divided in $L and C$. Hence, total stored energy $\begin{aligned}& E=\frac{1}{2} \times \text{ maximum stored energy$ in $either } L \text{ o }\\
& \Rightarrow \frac{1}{2} L I²=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2} L I_0^2 \Rightarrow I²=\left(\frac{I_0}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2 \Rightarrow I=\frac{I_0}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{aligned}$ We know that, $I=I_0 \sin \omega t \begin{aligned}& \Rightarrow \quad \frac{I_0}{\sqrt{2}}= I_0 \sin \omega t \Rightarrow \sin \frac{\pi}{4}=\sin \frac{2 \pi}{T} \cdot t \\
& \Rightarrow \quad \frac{\pi}{4}=\frac{2 \pi}{T} \cdot t \Rightarrow t=\frac{T}{8}=\frac{2 \pi \sqrt{L C}}{8}=\frac{\pi \sqrt{L C}}{4}
\end{aligned}$

Given, magnetic flux density, B=1 $\mathrm{ Wb}$/ $\mathrm{m}^2 Number$ of turns, N=80$
Area of coil, $A=0.01 $\mathrm{ m}^2$\Delta$ t=0.2 \mathrm{ s}$ According to Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction, induced emf $\begin{aligned}
|$ e| & =$\frac{N \Delta \phi}{\Delta t}=$ N $\cdot \frac{B A}{\Delta t} \quad[\because \Delta \phi=$ B A] \\
& =$\frac{80 \times 1 \times 0.01}{0.2}=4 \mathrm{ V}
\end{aligned}$

Induced emf, $e=\frac{-L d I}{d t} \begin{aligned}
\therefore$& 100 &=$\frac{-L[0-4]}{0.1}$\\
\Rightarrow & L &=$\frac{100 \times 0.1}{4}$\\
&=2.5 $\mathrm{H}
\end{aligned}$
